 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Squeeze Page Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a squeeze page?\", \"a\": \"A squeeze page is a landing page with one goal: getting a visitor's email address. It offers something in return, such as a guide or a discount code, and asks for little beyond the address. Marketers also call it an opt-in page.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you create a squeeze page?\", \"a\": \"Choose the freebie first, then write a headline that names it. With Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-landing-page-builder'>AI landing page builder</a>, one prompt describes the page and what should happen after signup, and Emergent builds both. Any later change is another message.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is an opt-in page the same as a squeeze page?\", \"a\": \"Mostly, yes. Both names describe a page whose job is an email signup, and many marketers use them interchangeably. Some keep opt-in page for any signup page, including a <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-landing-page-builder/webinar-landing-page'>webinar landing page</a> where registering also adds the visitor to a list. A squeeze page is the strict form, with one offer and one form.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a website squeeze page?\", \"a\": \"It is a squeeze page that lives on your main site, usually on the same domain, with the header navigation and footer links taken off. Visitors arrive from a post or an ad and see only the offer and the form. Emergent can host it on your own domain; custom domains are included and use credits.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a squeeze page funnel?\", \"a\": \"It is the path that starts at the opt-in. The squeeze page funnel runs from the signup form to a thank you page that delivers the freebie and often makes a small paid offer. Follow-up emails come next, and each step should point to the following one.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are good squeeze page examples?\", \"a\": \"The squeeze page examples worth studying pair a specific freebie with a short form. A meal plan or a first-order discount code works because the visitor knows what arrives after the click. Borrow the structure and write your own words.\"}, {\"q\": \"Where can I find squeeze page templates?\", \"a\": \"Page builders and swipe-file sites keep galleries of squeeze page templates, and they help with layout. The offer and the headline still have to come from you. Emergent skips the template: describe the offer and the audience, and the page is built around them.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a squeeze page template include?\", \"a\": \"At minimum, a headline that names the freebie and a form that asks for the email address. The button should say what arrives next, such as Send my checklist, rather than Submit. A cover image of the freebie can sit beside the form. Leave out menus and outbound links, because every extra link is a way off the page.\"}, {\"q\": \"What makes a high-converting squeeze page?\", \"a\": \"Good squeeze page design starts with an offer specific enough to want and a form short enough to finish on a phone. Proof helps, such as a subscriber count or a line from a reader. Then compare signups against visits before and after each edit.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I make a squeeze page for free?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Emergent is free to start, and the free tier covers building and publishing a squeeze page with its signup form. A custom domain uses credits. What sending the follow-up emails costs depends on the email provider you connect.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
